369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (How effectively can textual information be used to identify mental-health-related sentiment in social media posts? What additional information can visual content provide for such classification? Does combining textual and visual features improve classification performance compared with individual modalities?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (Can an AI-driven multimodal framework combining textual and visual information improve sentiment-based mental health monitoring on social media compared with unimodal approaches?)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (The project aims to study existing AI-based approaches for social-media sentiment and mental-health analysis and develop suitable preprocessing methods for textual and visual data. It will develop a multimodal framework that combines text and image representations for classification. The performance of the multimodal approach will be compared with text-only and image-only models. Standard metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score will be used for evaluation.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (The project focuses on computational analysis of textual and visual information obtained from social media content. It will include data preprocessing, feature extraction, multimodal feature fusion, classification, and performance evaluation using Artificial Intelligence and Deep Learning techniques. The proposed framework will focus on identifying mental-health-related sentiment or patterns rather than providing clinical diagnosis. The system is intended as a research and monitoring support tool and not as a replacement for qualified mental-health professionals.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
