<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 153 0 R
/T (Theme 3: Multimodal Sentiment and Mental Health Analysis)
/E (Theme 3: Multimodal Sentiment and Mental Health Analysis)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 153 0 R
/E (Multimodal analysis combines information from different data types to obtain a richer representation of social media content. Existing approaches combine textual and visual features using techniques such as feature fusion, attention mechanisms, and multimodal learning architectures. Such approaches can capture complementary information from multiple modalities and may improve classification performance. However, challenges remain in effectively combining modalities and handling differences or conflicts between textual and visual information.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (4)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (Based on the identified research gap, the proposed work will combine textual and visual information from social media posts within a single analytical framework. Textual features will be extracted using suitable NLP techniques, while visual features will be obtained using an appropriate computer-vision model. The extracted features will then be fused and supplied to a classification model for generating the final output. The proposed approach will be evaluated against unimodal approaches to determine the usefulness of multimodal information.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (Existing research demonstrates the usefulness of both text-based and multimodal approaches for analysing social media content related to sentiment and mental health. However, effectively integrating textual and visual information remains challenging because different modalities may contain complementary or conflicting emotional cues. There is also a need to evaluate multimodal approaches against individual modality-based models using consistent performance measures. This project addresses this gap by developing and evaluating a practical AI-driven framework for multimodal sentiment-based mental health monitoring.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
